<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/free-will-and-determinism">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Philosophy. You need nothing from earlier lessons — only the
    feeling, which you have dozens of times a day, that you <em>could have done otherwise</em>,
    and a willingness to put that feeling under the lamp. Everything else is built here, step
    by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state what determinism claims — and what it does not claim, since it is confused with fatalism and with perfect prediction more often than with anything else;</li>
    <li>run the standard dilemma that traps any attempt to locate free will in physics: determined feels unfree, random feels unfree;</li>
    <li>explain the three great positions — hard determinism, libertarianism, compatibilism — and what each must pay;</li>
    <li>reconstruct the consequence argument, the sharpest weapon on the incompatibilist's side;</li>
    <li>explain the compatibilist alternative: freedom as acting on your own reasons without coercion, Frankfurt's second-order desires, and the attack on "could have done otherwise";</li>
    <li>say what actually depends on the answer — criminal punishment, praise, blame — and what the Libet experiments do and do not show.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever you hear "I could have done otherwise", stop and ask <em>which sense
    of "could"</em> is meant. The entire three-century debate lives inside that one word, and
    most confusion about free will is undiagnosed ambiguity about it.
  </p>

  <h2>Try to catch yourself choosing</h2>
  <p>
    Raise your right arm. Now do it again — but this time, watch what happens between the
    deciding and the raising. You will find nothing to watch: the intention simply arrives,
    already yours, already acting. You cannot catch the <em>choosing</em> itself, only its
    results. Now a harder question: why did that intention exist at all? Something made it —
    your mood, this paragraph, causes in your brain you will never inspect. And yet nothing
    about the experience felt forced. That combination — an action with causes you cannot
    see, which nevertheless feels entirely yours — is the raw material of one of philosophy's
    oldest problems.
  </p>
  <p>
    The problem matters far beyond the seminar room. Courts punish people because they assume
    the punished <em>could have obeyed the law</em>. Parents praise children, employers reward
    workers, friends forgive friends — all of this talk of desert seems to presuppose that
    people are the true origins of their actions. If that presupposition fails, an entire
    legal and moral order has to be rethought. So philosophers have mapped the positions with
    unusual care, and the map is the subject of this chapter.
  </p>

  <h2>Determinism: the threat, stated precisely</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Determinism</strong> is the thesis that, given the complete state of the world at
    one time and the laws of nature, only one future is possible. Its classic image is
    Laplace's demon: an intellect that knew the position of every particle and every law
    could, in principle, read the future as unambiguously as the past. Notice three things
    the thesis does <em>not</em> say. It does not say your choices don't matter — your
    deliberations are links in the causal chain, and they may be exactly what produces the
    outcome. It does not say the future is <em>predetermined</em> in the sense of being
    written down somewhere, independent of what anyone does. And it does not say prediction
    must be practical: real systems are usually too complicated to compute, which is why we
    cannot forecast next month's weather even in a largely deterministic world.
  </p>
  <p>
    Determinism must also be distinguished from <strong>fatalism</strong> — the belief that
    what will happen will happen <em>no matter what you do</em>, the "whatever will be, will
    be" doctrine. The two are opposites on the point that matters. Fatalism says the doctor
    will save you regardless of whether you visit her; determinism says you will be saved
    <em>because</em> you visited, and had you not, the future would have differed. Under
    determinism your efforts are causally effective — indeed necessary. Spinoza put the
    diagnosis of our confusion beautifully in the 1670s: men believe themselves free
    <em>"because they are conscious of their volitions and ignorant of the causes by which
    those volitions are determined"</em> — we feel free because we can't see our own gears.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if determinism is true, nothing I do matters — so why
  get out of bed?" That is fatalism, not determinism, and it inverts the view. On
  determinism, what happens happens <em>through</em> your deliberations and efforts, not
  despite them; the argument that "the future is fixed, so my action makes no difference"
  forgets that your action is part of what fixes it. The determinist does not believe in
  doing nothing; the determinist believes that deciding to do nothing would also have
  causes.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "determinism means science will one day predict
  everything you do." Determinism is a claim about what <em>fixes</em> the future, not about
  what can be <em>computed</em>. Perfect prediction would require perfect knowledge of every
  particle — impossible for any observer inside the universe — and chaotic systems make even
  approximate long-range forecasting fail. Laplace's demon is a definition of what the laws
  would imply, not an engineering roadmap.</p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the threat, the denials, and the dominant compromise" widget="equationStep" config={fwCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards are not four opinions of equal standing — they
    are a logical sequence. Card 1 states the threat. Cards 2 and 3 accept that threat on
    opposite terms: the hard determinist keeps determinism and gives up free will; the
    libertarian keeps free will and denies determinism where it counts. Card 4 refuses the
    framing altogether, and that refusal is the majority position among professional
    philosophers (about 59 per cent in the 2020 PhilPapers survey). Watch how much work the
    word "freedom" has to do in each card — that is the real variable of the debate.
  </p>

  <h2>The quantum rescue that fails</h2>
  <p>
    A common first response is to invoke quantum mechanics: physics, since the 1920s,
    describes outcomes at the smallest scales as genuinely probabilistic, so maybe the future
    is not fixed after all. But this rescue fails on inspection. If your "choice" is the
    result of a quantum dice-roll inside a synapse, the roll is <em>random</em> — and a random
    choice is no more yours than a determined one. Randomness is not control. Imagine a
    shuffling machine that sometimes injects pure noise into your decisions: that is not
    freedom, it is a slot machine in the skull. This is the standard dilemma facing any
    attempt to locate free will in physics: <strong>determined</strong> feels unfree,
    <strong>random</strong> feels unfree, and every event is some combination of the two.
    That dilemma is the engine of the whole debate — every position below is, in one way or
    another, an attempt to escape it.
  </p>

  <h2>The oldest form: God's foreknowledge</h2>
  <p>
    There is also <em>theological</em> determinism to consider, and it produced the debate's
    oldest form. If God is <strong>omniscient</strong> — knows everything, including the
    future — then God already knows what you will do tomorrow. But then, it seems, you cannot
    do anything else: if you could, God's knowledge would be wrong, which is impossible. The
    medieval answer (Boethius, sixth century) is that God is <em>timeless</em>, outside the
    flow of time altogether: God doesn't "foresee" your choice, God simply sees it, as you
    see a bird crossing the sky — and watching does not force the bird. A God
    <em>everlasting</em> — existing within time, moment by moment — has a harder time with
    the argument. This is the problem of the compatibility of divine foreknowledge and human
    freedom, and it is structurally identical to the scientific one: perfect prediction
    replaces perfect laws, and the question is the same. It is why this lesson belongs to the
    philosophy of God as much as to the philosophy of mind.
  </p>

  <h2>Hard determinism: accept the consequence</h2>
  <p>
    The most direct response is to accept determinism and deny free will. <strong>Hard
    determinists</strong> such as Spinoza, Baron d'Holbach and, in our own time, Derk
    Pereboom, argue that the popular conception of freedom — that you could have done
    otherwise in exactly the same circumstances, that you are the ultimate origin of your
    actions — is simply false, and that we should live honestly with that. Pereboom's phrase
    for the resulting life is "living without free will": we would still imprison people, but
    for <em>protection, deterrence and rehabilitation</em>, not retribution. Punishment as
    "deserved suffering" would be abandoned, the way public flogging was abandoned.
  </p>
  <p>
    The view has an uncomfortable cost that its opponents press hard: ordinary moral life
    seems to collapse. When a friend keeps a promise, praise seems appropriate; when someone
    betrays you, anger seems natural. The hard determinist must say these reactions are, in
    some deep sense, misfiring — or at least that their "desert" version should be dropped.
    Some philosophers (P. F. Strawson, in his 1962 essay <em>Freedom and Resentment</em>)
    replied that this is psychologically impossible: reactive emotions like gratitude and
    resentment are not optional equipment we can put down once we believe a theory. Whether
    hard determinism is livable is itself a live philosophical question.
  </p>

  <h2>Libertarian free will: break the chain</h2>
  <p>
    The opposite response accepts that free will exists and concludes that determinism must
    be false — at least where human agents are concerned. <strong>Libertarians</strong> (in
    this technical sense — nothing to do with the political position) argue that when a
    person makes a genuinely free choice, that event is <em>not determined</em> by prior
    causes, yet is not random either: it is caused by the <em>agent</em> herself, a special
    kind of causation. Roderick Chisholm called this "agent causation" — the person, not an
    event, is the cause.
  </p>
  <p>
    The immediate problem is the one already met: how does this dodge the dilemma? If the
    choice is undetermined, isn't it just random with better branding? Libertarians answer
    that the agent <em>controls</em> the choice in a way no physical process can — you settle
    it. Critics answer that this is a name for the mystery, not a solution to it, and that it
    sits poorly with neuroscience, where mental events track brain events with increasing
    precision. Still, libertarianism has formidable defenders (Robert Kane argues that
    randomness in the brain's decision-making can be <em>amplified and settled by</em> an
    agent's own reasons and efforts, giving undetermined but self-controlled choice), and it
    preserves the strongest reading of "could have done otherwise".
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the dilemma splits the field in two, and each side has now
    spoken. The hard determinist accepts determinism and pays with moral desert; the
    libertarian keeps desert and pays with a mysterious power of agent causation. What
    neither has yet faced is the argument that makes the incompatibilist case feel
    airtight — and the escape route that does not deny it.
  </p>

  <h2>The consequence argument: the strongest card of the incompatibilist</h2>
  <p>
    Why do so many philosophers think determinism rules out the popular freedom? Because of
    a short, sharp argument published by Peter van Inwagen in 1983, called the
    <strong>consequence argument</strong>. It runs: (1) Nobody has power over the laws of
    nature. (2) Nobody has power over the state of the world in the distant past — including
    the state before they were born. (3) But if determinism is true, our present actions are
    an inevitable consequence of those two things, the laws plus the past. (4) If you have no
    power over X, and no power over Y, and X and Y jointly entail Z, you have no power over
    Z. So: nobody has power over their own present actions.
  </p>
  <p>
    A vivid way to see the point: imagine jumping into a time machine and arriving in the
    year 1500 — you cannot affect the past you find there. But if determinism is true, your
    actions <em>today</em> are as fixed a consequence of 1500 as tomorrow's sunrise. Your
    choices are, in van Inwagen's phrase, "a consequence of the laws of nature and events in
    the remote past", and that consequence is not up to you. The argument's premises feel
    almost undeniable — which is why the compatibilist's escape route is not to deny them but
    to argue that the conclusion, however true, does not mean what it seems to mean.
  </p>

  <h2>Compatibilism: redefining the freedom that matters</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Compatibilism</strong> — associated with Thomas Hobbes and David Hume in the
    eighteenth century and dominant today (about 59% of professional philosophers in the 2020
    PhilPapers survey) — agrees with the hard determinist that determinism is true, and with
    the libertarian that free will exists. Its move is radical: the two were never in
    conflict, because "free will" never meant <em>escaping causation</em>. It meant something
    entirely available inside a determined world.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classical Humean version: an action is free when it flows from your own desires and
    decisions, <em>without external constraint</em>. A prisoner free to walk about his cell
    has compatibilist freedom within it; when chained, he doesn't. Notice this is a
    distinction that determinism cannot erase — between the determined action of someone
    acting on their own reasons, and the determined action of someone coerced at gunpoint,
    there is all the difference in the world, and it is the difference we actually care
    about.
  </p>
  <p>
    Harry Frankfurt sharpened this in 1971 with <strong>second-order desires</strong>: you
    are free when you want your wants. An addict wants the drug but does not want to want it
    — his will is divided, and he is unfree. A person whose first-order desires line up with
    what she endorses on reflection has the only freedom worth wanting. Frankfurt then went
    further in 1969 and attacked the deep assumption behind the consequence argument: the
    "principle of alternate possibilities", that you are responsible only if you could have
    done otherwise. Consider a neuroscientist with a device that will force you to vote for a
    certain candidate if you show any sign of voting otherwise — but you vote that way
    entirely on your own, and the device never activates. You could not have done otherwise
    (the device was waiting), yet you acted freely and are fully responsible. Alternate
    possibilities, Frankfurt argued, are not what freedom is made of; <em>responsiveness to
    reasons</em> is. Compatibilists like Daniel Dennett built on this: freedom worth having
    is the capacity to respond to reasons, foresee consequences and act on your own
    evaluations — abilities that exist, and exist more or less of, in a determined world, and
    that a random quantum dice-roll would only spoil.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> compatibilism is not a cowardly fudge that "redefines the
  problem away". It is a substantive claim that the freedom presupposed by law and morality —
  acting on your own reasons without coercion — is real, well-defined, and unaffected by
  determinism. The dispute with the incompatibilist is about which concept deserves the name
  "free will", and that dispute is fought with arguments about desert, manipulation and the
  foundations of blame, not with dictionary tricks.</p>

  <h2>Why the answer has consequences</h2>
  <p>
    The stakes show up immediately in criminal law. <strong>Retributive</strong> theories of
    punishment — the person deserves to suffer because they chose wrongly — presuppose deep
    freedom. If that presupposition fails, punishment can only be justified
    <em>consequentially</em>: deterrence, incapacitation, rehabilitation. Notice that our
    actual law already carves out excuses that look exactly like the compatibilist's
    criteria: insanity, coercion, infancy and duress excuse because the agent was not
    properly responding to reasons. The law, arguably, is already compatibilist.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stakes also show up in the lab. <strong>Benjamin Libet's experiments</strong> (1983)
    are routinely cited as disproving free will: participants watching a fast clock reported
    deciding to move at about 200 ms, but a "readiness potential" — a build-up of brain
    activity — preceded their report by over half a second, and headlines announced that the
    brain decides before the conscious mind does. Libet himself proposed a "free won't" — a
    conscious veto — to rescue some role for the agent.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that the Libet experiments "disproved free will". They
  showed a brain signal preceding a reported decision in a highly artificial task —
  spontaneous finger flexions, not deliberative choice. Later work (Aaron Schurger and
  colleagues, 2012) suggests the readiness potential may be ordinary background noise
  crossing a threshold rather than the start of a decision, and the reported timing is a
  retrospective judgement with errors of its own. The experiments constrain certain pictures
  of the mind; they settle nothing about the argument between determinist and compatibilist,
  which was never about millisecond timings in the first place.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State determinism, then state fatalism, and mark the exact point where the two views part company.</li>
    <li>Why doesn't quantum indeterminacy rescue the popular idea of free will?</li>
    <li>Reconstruct the consequence argument in your own words. Which premise would a libertarian deny, and why?</li>
    <li>In Frankfurt's voting case, why does the neuroscientist's standby device fail to remove responsibility?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Determinism: one future follows necessarily from the past
    plus the laws. Fatalism: the outcome occurs no matter what you do. They part company over
    the role of your actions: determinism makes your efforts necessary links in producing the
    outcome; fatalism makes them irrelevant. (2) Because the alternative to determination is
    randomness, and a randomly injected choice is no more <em>yours</em> than a forced one —
    the dilemma: determined feels unfree, random feels unfree. (3) No power over the laws; no
    power over the remote past; if the present follows inevitably from those, no power over
    the present either. A libertarian typically denies the third step — the "inevitability"
    link — by denying that agent-caused choices are deterministic consequences of the past.
    (4) Because the device never activated: you voted from your own reasons, so your action
    had the history responsiveness-to-reasons that Frankfurt thinks freedom consists in.
    Alternate possibilities were unavailable, but they were not what mattered.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Determinism: the past plus the laws fix one future. It is not fatalism (your actions matter, causally) and not a prediction programme (fixing is not computing).</li>
    <li>The dilemma: determined feels unfree, random feels unfree — so locating freedom "in the physics" fails on both horns.</li>
    <li>Hard determinism accepts the threat and drops retributive desert; whether the resulting life is livable (Strawson's reactive attitudes) is disputed.</li>
    <li>Libertarianism posits agent causation — undetermined but controlled choice; critics call it a name for the mystery.</li>
    <li>The consequence argument (van Inwagen): laws + remote past entail your actions, and you are powerless over the first two.</li>
    <li>Compatibilism (Hume, Frankfurt, Dennett — the majority view): freedom is acting on your own reasons without coercion; alternate possibilities are not what responsibility requires.</li>
    <li>The Libet experiments constrain mind–brain pictures but do not settle the metaphysical debate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This problem sits at the junction of three roads in the atlas. The philosophy-of-mind
    lesson continues into the questions this page raised about brain events and mental
    events; the metaphysics lesson asks what kind of causation exists in the first place; and
    every lesson in ethics quietly borrows the answer you adopt here, because blame, praise
    and desert are currency the moral theories spend without examining. The theological thread
    — omniscience and freedom — returns in lessons on the concept of God.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Manipulation cases are the compatibilist's hardest test. Imagine a neuroscientist who
    <em>implants</em> your desire — engineers your values so that you then act on them
    "freely", by Frankfurt's own criteria, with no coercion at all. Intuitively you are not
    responsible, yet determinism says your desires were likewise implanted — by heredity and
    upbringing. This is the <strong>manipulation argument</strong>, and compatibilists have
    answered in two ways: by finding a principled difference between machine implantation and
    ordinary causation of character (Dennett argues there is none that matters, so our
    intuition misfires), or by adding a "history" condition to freedom — desires must be
    acquired through reasonably responsive processes. The debate is unresolved and is where
    the live action is.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second frontier is the <strong>hard-incompatibilist</strong> position of Pereboom: even
    if determinism is false (say quantum indeterminacy is real), the randomness in our neural
    machinery still rules out ultimate desert — so neither determinism nor indeterminism
    secures the strong freedom. Against him, compatibilists reply that "ultimate"
    origination may be an incoherent demand, like asking an author to write a book that was
    not written out of anything. And a third strand is empirical: studies by Vohs and
    Schooler found that reading anti-free-will passages made participants cheat more —
    suggesting belief in free will itself does moral work, whatever the metaphysical truth.
    The 150-year-old verdict of William James — that the problem is a "quandary" every
    serious mind must face — still stands; but the majority view has shifted to a subtle one:
    the universe may be a machine, and we may still be responsible inside it.
  </p>

